                <pb facs="1086" n="990" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg990"/>

                <head>No. 473.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Maynard to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-10-13"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 13, 1879</date>.
                        (Received November 8.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 348.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I had the honor in my dispatch No. 341, of
                    September 12, 1879, to mention the absence of Her Britannic Majesty’s ambassador
                    on a trip to Syria and the Holy Land.</p>
                <p>After an absence of a month he returned early this morning, stopping at the
                    summer palace of the embassy on the Upper Bosphorous. I have not yet seen him,
                    but I understand he and his suite came back in robust health.</p>
                <p>Reports of the journey have been published in the city papers, of which I inclose
                    a selection.</p>
                <p>The United States consuls at Beirut and Jerusalem have addressed communications
                    to the consul-general on the same topic, the substance of which is transcribed
                    for inclosure. The impression made upon these officials will appear to be
                    dissimilar.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>HORACE MAYNARD.</signed></closer>





